Sources, transparency and final control
An AI answer should not be trusted only because it sounds convincing. An SME should be able to see where important claims come from. Our platform therefore links important claims to their original sources and records the organisation or author behind the information. NIST describes transparency and accountability as important parts of trustworthy AI. 
Sources are also checked before they are used. We look at who published the information, how recent it is, whether evidence is provided and whether the source is relevant to the question. Official organisations, legislation and recognised research institutions are preferred when they directly support a claim.
The platform may help search, compare information and create a first draft, but it does not make the final publication decision. AI drafts; the team checks. Before publication, a team member checks whether the claim actually matches the original source, whether important context is missing and whether confidential or personal information has accidentally been included.
Transparency also matters when AI is used towards other people. The EU AI Act contains specific transparency requirements for certain AI systems, and those Article 50 requirements have applied since 2 August 2026. This does not mean every SME use of AI has exactly the same legal requirements, but it shows why businesses should know when, where and how AI is being used.

